{{also|Nicely}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|nice|ly}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈnʌɪsli/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-nicely.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|er|more}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[fastidiously|Fastidiously]]; [[carefully]]. {{defdate|16th|18th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|III|xii|passage=He lookt askew with his mistrustfull eyes, / And '''nicely''' trode, as thornes lay in his way, / Or that the flore to shrinke he did auyse [...].}}
# [[precisely|Precisely]]; with fine [[discernment]] or [[judgement]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1883|author=Robert Louis Stevenson|title=Treasure Island
|passage=All told, we had scarce two miles to run; but the navigation was delicate, the entrance to this northern anchorage was not only narrow and shoal, but lay east and west, so that the schooner must be '''nicely''' handled to be got in.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1926|author=w:Ford Madox Ford|title=A Man Could Stand Up—|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2012|series=Parade's End|page=580
|passage=An army – especially in peace time – is a very complex and '''nicely''' adjusted affair […].}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Thomas Penn|title=Winter King|page=59|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2012
|passage=Henry's carefully calibrated public appearances would present him as the wellspring of honour, justice and power, the unknowable, all-seeing sovereign who, as the Milanese ambassador Soncino '''nicely''' observed, appeared in public ‘like one at the top of a tower looking on at what is passing in the plain’.}}
# [[pleasantly|Pleasantly]]; [[satisfactorily]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* '''1986-1987''', [[w:Lou Sullivan|Lou Sullivan]], personal diary, quoted in '''2019''', Ellis Martin, Zach Ozma (editors), ''We Both Laughed In Pleasure''
#*: He was a sweet boy, but became the kind of man I can do '''nicely''' without.

